      "message": "This CL is a replacement for I595acaf5e798700b5d376115479774eb2bdbfccd, which requires a constexpr vector constructor.\n\nI debugged clang++ to see why this new workaround worked. TLDR: The requires clause in `std::reverse_iterator`\u0027s `operator\u003d\u003d` references `__x` and `__y` variables, and Clang checks to see if these variables are captured by a lambda somewhere. During that check, Clang seemingly fails to notice that the variables are declared as parameters to the `operator\u003d\u003d` function, and instead searches through a list of function scopes, and then crashes when it doesn\u0027t find the variable declarations. If `size_lambda` captures nothing by default, then Clang halts its search at the lambda, because it sees that `size_lambda` can\u0027t capture `__x` and `__y`.\n\nDetailed analysis:\n\nSee https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/89496#issuecomment-2067642681 for reference.\n\nSomewhere in calculateConstraintSatisfaction (C++20 concepts/constraints handling), Clang eventually calls NeedToCaptureVariable for the reference to `other` from the `requires` clause:\n\n```\ntemplate \u003cclass Iter\u003e\nstruct RevIter {\n  Iter current;\n  constexpr explicit RevIter(Iter x) : current(x) {}\n  inline constexpr bool operator\u003d\u003d(const RevIter\u003cIter\u003e\u0026 other) const\n    requires requires {\n      //{ current \u003d\u003d other.current } -\u003e std::convertible_to\u003cbool\u003e;\n      { other };\n    }\n  {\n    return true;\n  }\n};\n```\n\nClang then tries to actually capture the variable:\n\n```\nbool Sema::NeedToCaptureVariable(ValueDecl *Var, SourceLocation Loc) {\n  QualType CaptureType;\n  QualType DeclRefType;\n  return !tryCaptureVariable(Var, Loc, TryCapture_Implicit, SourceLocation(),\n                             /*BuildAndDiagnose\u003d*/false, CaptureType,\n                             DeclRefType, nullptr);\n}\n```\n\ntryCaptureVariable skips global variables (exception for OpenMP only):\n\n```\n  // Capture global variables if it is required to use private copy of this\n  // variable.\n  bool IsGlobal \u003d !VD-\u003ehasLocalStorage();\n  if (IsGlobal \u0026\u0026\n      !(LangOpts.OpenMP \u0026\u0026 isOpenMPCapturedDecl(Var, /*CheckScopeInfo\u003d*/true,\n                                                MaxFunctionScopesIndex)))\n    return true;\n```\n\nThe variable is local, so tryCaptureVariable ought to be able to find where `other` is declared, and it looks for it by walking up function scopes from inner to outer. However, the `Sema::FunctionScopes` vector doesn\u0027t have the `operator\u003d\u003d` parameter declaration in it -- it only has a single entry for the lambda scope in CrashFunc.\n\nIn principle, maybe tryCaptureVariable ought to exit here (or something like it):\n\n```\n    // If the variable is declared in the current context, there is no need to\n    // capture it.\n    if (IsInScopeDeclarationContext \u0026\u0026\n        FunctionScopesIndex \u003d\u003d MaxFunctionScopesIndex \u0026\u0026 VarDC \u003d\u003d DC)\n      return true;\n```\n\nHowever, because it doesn\u0027t look at the `operator\u003d\u003d` parameters, it instead checks the lambda. If the lambda captures are `[\u0026]` or `[\u003d]`, then tryCaptureVariable assumes the lambda _does_ capture `other`, and continues looking for the declaration. It decrements FunctionScopesIndex (from 0 to -1/UINT_MAX), and crashes on the next iteration of the loop:\n\n```\n    if (!FunctionScopes.empty())\n      LSI \u003d dyn_cast_or_null\u003cLambdaScopeInfo\u003e(\n          FunctionScopes[FunctionScopesIndex]);\n```\n\nHowever, if no variables are captured by default in the lambda, then `CSI-\u003eImpCaptureStyle` is `CapturingScopeInfo::ImpCap_None`, and the function returns true, using this code path instead:\n\n```\n    if (CSI-\u003eImpCaptureStyle \u003d\u003d CapturingScopeInfo::ImpCap_None \u0026\u0026 !Explicit) {\n      // No capture-default, and this is not an explicit capture\n      // so cannot capture this variable.\n      ...\n      return true;\n    }\n```\n\nReturning true seems to indicate that the variable can\u0027t be captured by a lambda, which is correct because `other` is local to `operator\u003d\u003d`. It is a reference to a parameter of the lambda.",
